# Linux Packet Layout (No Manual Construction)

When fixing **packet struct layout** on Linux (e.g. AU_LOGIN_RES 839 vs 795 bytes; client expects Windows layout):

1. **Read first:** [docs/LINUX_RECEIVE_PACKET_FIX_LOG.md](docs/LINUX_RECEIVE_PACKET_FIX_LOG.md) (section 19 and Rules for Future Fixes).
2. **No manual packet construction ever.** Do not build packet payloads at fixed byte offsets in .cpp (e.g. manual writes to p+0, p+65, etc.). Rejected: it does not scale and is not a real fix.
3. **Find a global fix** that applies to all packets without modifying every single packet. Try (in order): compiler flag (e.g. `-fpack-struct` on Linux) and fix any reference-to-packed-field breakage; `#pragma ms_struct` (GCC/Clang, Linux x86_64) for MS-compatible layout; stronger `#pragma pack(push,1)`/`pack(pop)`; or struct/ABI change that makes `__attribute__((packed))` or pack effective for protocol structs.
4. **What already failed:** POD base, `__attribute__((packed))` on sAU_LOGIN_RES/sSERVER_INFO, composition instead of inheritance — sizeof stayed 839 on Linux. **Option 1 (global):** #pragma ms_struct on sNTLPACKETHEADER and sSERVER_INFO + -mms-bitfields on NtlShared did not yield 795; ms_struct on derived protocol structs errors (base class). See fix log 19c. Document new attempts in the fix log.
5. **After** any new attempt, append to the log: what was tried, result (worked/failed), and update "What Works" / "What Failed" if needed.
